-- 11: error handling, three candidate styles side by side.
-- This is still map fog ("Error handling"); nothing is decided. Fixed points:
--   * Limit Faults are outside the error model: they can't be caught, and the
--     Segment rolls back (ADR 0006).
--   * An error inside a Guard skips the clause (issue #4).
--   * Ordinary errors the sandbox contract already names: `send` to a full
--     mailbox, calling a revoked Capability, and a failed `send … and wait`.
--   * Plus the ordinary ones: `"007" + 1`, `5 kg + 3 m`, divide by zero,
--     `"lots" as number`, writing character 20 of "short", Text Pattern search
--     on a value that isn't text.
--
use decodeJson from json

-- Error value sketch: plain data, so it can be matched and Destructured.
--   {code: "not a number", message: "…", at: {line: 12, handler: "addUp"}, value: "lots"}
-- ?? code as text ("not a number") or a symbol-ish name (not_a_number)?

------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A. try / catch (SenseTalk, _hyperscript)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

on addUp csvLine
  put 0 into sum
  repeat for each part in the items of csvLine
    try
      add part as number to sum
    catch {code: "not a number", value: v}
      tell log to write "skipping " & v
    catch e
      throw e                                  -- ALT: rethrow
    end try
  end repeat
  return sum
end addUp
-- Catch clauses are Destructuring heads, the same machinery as Handler Clauses.
-- That's the best thing about this style here.

on fetchOrFallback url
  try
    ask http to get url and wait
    return decodeJson(it's body as text)
  catch {code: "capability revoked"}
    return nothing
  catch {code: "http", status: s} where s >= 500
    wait 1 s
    ask http to get url and wait               -- one retry
    return decodeJson(it's body as text)
  end try
end fetchOrFallback

-- Raising your own
on withdraw amount
  if amount > balance then
    throw {code: "insufficient funds", balance: balance, wanted: amount}
    -- ALT: fail "insufficient funds" with {balance: balance}
  end if
  subtract amount from balance
end withdraw

-- ?? Rollback: a caught error does NOT roll back Script Variable changes made
--    before it inside the `try` (only Limit Faults roll back a Segment).
--    Is that obvious to a reader? `subtract … from balance` then an error
--    later in the same `try` leaves balance changed.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- B. Tagged results (Elixir {:ok, v} / {:error, e})
------------------------------------------------------------------------

on parseQty text
  if text can be a number then return {ok: text as number}
  return {error: "not a number", value: text}
end parseQty

on addUpB csvLine
  put 0 into sum
  repeat for each part in the items of csvLine
    match parseQty(part)
      when {ok: n} then add n to sum
      when {error: why} then tell log to write "skipping " & part & ": " & why
    end match
  end repeat
  return sum
end addUpB

-- ADR 0017: tagged results are only a Script convention, Capabilities raise,
-- and `with` is deferred, so `fetchB` and `loadConfig` are gone.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- C. Let it crash (Erlang): an error ends the Run; the Host (or a Script-level
--    handler) is told. No try in the language at all.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

on handle {type: "invoice", amount: a}
  add a as number to total                     -- "lots" → this Run ends here
  send booked with a to ledgerWatcher
end handle

-- A Script-level handler that sees any Run that ended in an error
on error {code: c, message: m, at: loc}, during msg
  -- was `at: where`: `where` is reserved (the Guard word), so it can't be a name.
  tell log to write "handling " & the name of msg & " failed: " & m
  send alert with {code: c, message: m} to ops
  -- ?? Runs after the failed Run ends, as a new Run with its own budgets.
  --    Can't resume, can't retry in place. Changes before the error stand
  --    (again: only Limit Faults roll back).
end error
-- ALT: on failed msg because e

-- How the error reaches a `send … and wait` sender (applies to all three):
on submit order
  send review with order to approvals and wait
  -- A: the receiver's error is thrown here, as {code: "remote error", reason: …}
  -- B: `it` is {error: {code: "remote error", reason: …}}
  -- C: this Run ends too, and the sender's `on error` gets it. ?? Cascading
  --    crashes across Scripts is the Erlang model without Erlang's supervisors.
end submit

------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Cleanup when a Run is cancelled (a `, replacing` Handler, Stop Script)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

on search term, replacing
  show spinner
  send query with term to index and wait
  showResults it
finally
  hide spinner
  -- ?? Runs on normal end, on error, and on cancellation by `replacing`.
  --    Must be bounded (its own small Fuel budget, no Suspension Points) so
  --    it can't weaken Stop Script. On a Limit Fault? Probably not at all.
end search
-- ALT: `on cancel … end cancel` inside the Handler
-- ALT: `ensure` (Ruby) / `always`

------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- My lean: A for the beginner layer, with catch clauses as Destructuring
-- heads and error values as plain maps; C's `on error` as the Script-level
-- backstop for anything uncaught; B left as a stdlib convention (Capabilities
-- could offer both), with `with` as an advanced-layer maybe.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
